    <body>
      <sec id="article-27231.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Pineal region tumors are rare lesions, representing about 1% of intracranial tumors in adults and up to 8% in children. The diversity of these tumors&#x000a0;reflects the variety of cell types within the pineal gland&#x000a0;and the numerous structures located nearby. These tumors may originate from the pineal parenchyma, represent germ cell neoplasms, arise from adjacent tissues, or develop as metastases from distant sites. Given their location, they frequently present with obstructive hydrocephalus or dorsal midbrain compression, requiring timely recognition and management.</p>
        <p>This activity provides a comprehensive review of the evaluation, classification, and management of pineal gland tumors. Learners explore diagnostic strategies, treatment algorithms, and prognostic factors, while also gaining insight into the critical role of the interprofessional team, including clinicians, advanced practitioners, nurses, pharmacists, and allied specialists, in optimizing patient-centered care, improving safety, and enhancing outcomes for individuals with these rare intracranial tumors.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the&#x000a0;clinical presentations of pineal region tumors.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement appropriate diagnostic pathways for pineal gland tumors.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply evidence-based treatment algorithms.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with interprofessional team members to optimize patient-centered care.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=27231&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=27231">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27231.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>The pineal gland is a small endocrine&#x000a0;structure, measuring 10&#x000a0;to 14 mm in length, located in the midline of the brain at the superior aspect of the posterior margin of the third ventricle.&#x000a0;On&#x000a0;coronal imaging, the gland lies below the splenium of the corpus callosum and above and posterior to the tectum of the midbrain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;The principal function of this gland is&#x000a0;the secretion of melatonin, a hormone that regulates circadian rhythms. The gland receives environmental light-dark cycle information and provides feedback to the central nervous system, thereby contributing to the regulation of biological rhythms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>The principal cells of the pineal gland are pinealocytes, which&#x000a0;constitute the&#x000a0;pineal parenchyma. Multiple pathologies are associated with this region due to the variety of cell types and structures adjacent to the pineal gland. Tumors of the pineal gland are classified into 3 subgroups&#x02014;pineal parenchyma tumors, germ cell tumors, and lesions originating from adjacent structures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r1">[1]</xref> Other lesions&#x000a0;encountered in this area include astrocytomas, oligodendrogliomas, pineal cysts, meningiomas, arachnoid cysts, ependymomas, chemodectomas, epidermoid cysts, dermoid cysts, metastasis, aneurysm of the vein of Galen, arteriovenous malformation, and cysticercosis.</p>
        <p>Important anatomical structures adjacent to the pineal gland include:<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Anteriorly: Third ventricle (pineal recess)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Anterosuperiorly: Habenular nuclei</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Superiorly: Internal cerebral veins, the vein of Galen (posteriorly), stria medullaris, splenium of the corpus callosum, and velum interpositum</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Posteroinferiorly: Superior cerebellar cistern</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Inferiorly: Superior colliculi of the midbrain</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Anteroinferiorly: Posterior commissure</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Differentiating tumors of the pineal region relies on integrating clinical presentation, imaging characteristics, tumor markers, and histopathology. Although many tumors present with overlapping symptoms such as hydrocephalus and Parinaud's syndrome, radiologic features, calcification patterns, and serum or cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) markers such as alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) and beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (&#x003b2;-hCG), help narrow the differential. Definitive diagnosis requires histological evaluation to guide management.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27231.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The etiology of pineal gland tumors is&#x000a0;determined by&#x000a0;their histopathological classification. These tumors are broadly divided into 2 major groups&#x02014;pineal parenchymal tumors and germ cell tumors.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Pineal parenchymal tumors</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <list list-type="bullet">
              <list-item>
                <p>Pineocytoma</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Pineal parenchymal tumor with intermediate differentiation</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Pineoblastoma</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Papillary tumor of the pineal region</p>
              </list-item>
            </list>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Germ cell tumors</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <list list-type="bullet">
              <list-item>
                <p>Germinomatous germ cell tumors</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <list list-type="bullet">
                  <list-item>
                    <p>Pineal germinoma</p>
                  </list-item>
                </list>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Nongerminomatous germ cell tumors</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <list list-type="bullet">
                  <list-item>
                    <p>Choriocarcinoma</p>
                  </list-item>
                  <list-item>
                    <p>Embryonal carcinoma</p>
                  </list-item>
                </list>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Yolk sac tumor (endodermal sinus tract tumor)</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Immature teratoma</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Mature teratoma</p>
              </list-item>
            </list>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27231.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Tumors of the pineal region are rare, comprising approximately 1% of all intracranial tumors in adults, but up to 8% of intracranial tumors in children.&#x000a0;The&#x000a0;heterogeneity of neoplasms in this region results in wide variation in their clinical characteristics and epidemiology.&#x000a0;The World Health Organization is one form of classification often used (see&#x000a0;<bold>Table.</bold>&#x000a0;2016 World Health Organization Classification).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-27231.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 1.&#x000a0;2016 World Health Organization Classification</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="20">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 25px;">
                <td style="width: 27px; height: 25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>#</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 132px; height: 25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Tumor Type</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 125px; height: 25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Age Range/Predominance</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 97.5625px; height: 25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Frequency/Contribution</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 119.438px; height: 25px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Markers/Notes</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 86.6667px;">
                <td style="width: 27px; height: 86.6667px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td>
                <td style="width: 132px; height: 86.6667px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pineocytoma</td>
                <td style="width: 125px; height: 86.6667px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>20-60 years</p></list-item><list-item><p>More common in females (M:F 0.6:1)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r4">[4]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 97.5625px; height: 86.6667px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>14%-30% of pineal parenchymal tumors</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 119.438px; height: 86.6667px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Well-differentiated&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r5">[5]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>WHO Grade 1</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 73px;">
                <td style="width: 27px; height: 73px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2</td>
                <td style="width: 132px; height: 73px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pineal parenchymal tumor with intermediate differentiation</td>
                <td style="width: 125px; height: 73px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>20-70 years</p></list-item><list-item><p>Slight female predominance&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 97.5625px; height: 73px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>20%-62% of pineal parenchymal tumors</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 119.438px; height: 73px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>WHO grade 2/3</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 88px;">
                <td style="width: 27px; height: 88px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3</td>
                <td style="width: 132px; height: 88px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Papillary tumor of the pineal region</td>
                <td style="width: 125px; height: 88px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>1-70 years</p></list-item><list-item><p>Peak in middle age&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r7">[7]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>No gender preference&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r4">[4]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 97.5625px; height: 88px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Rare</p></list-item><list-item><p>Broadest age range</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 119.438px; height: 88px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>WHO grade 2/3</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 177px;">
                <td style="width: 27px; height: 177px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4</td>
                <td style="width: 132px; height: 177px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pineoblastoma</td>
                <td style="width: 125px; height: 177px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Children</p></list-item><list-item><p>Slight female predominance (M:F 0.7:1)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r4">[4]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Associated with hereditary retinoblastoma (trilobar retinoblastoma)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 97.5625px; height: 177px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>24%-50% of pineal parenchymal tumors&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r7">[7]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 119.438px; height: 177px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Most aggressive</p></list-item><list-item><p>WHO grade 4&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 114px;">
                <td style="width: 27px; height: 114px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5</td>
                <td style="width: 132px; height: 114px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Germinoma</td>
                <td style="width: 125px; height: 114px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>&#x02264;20 years</p></list-item><list-item><p>Strong male predominance (M:F 13:1)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 97.5625px; height: 114px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Approximately 50% of pineal region tumors</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 119.438px; height: 114px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>CSF markers: &#x02191; placental alkaline phosphatase; &#x02191;&#x000a0;&#x003b2;-hCG&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r10">[10]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>WHO 9064/3 (malignant)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r10">[10]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 99px;">
                <td style="width: 27px; height: 99px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6</td>
                <td style="width: 132px; height: 99px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Choriocarcinoma</td>
                <td style="width: 125px; height: 99px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Variable</p></list-item><list-item><p>Often the pineal/suprasellar region</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 97.5625px; height: 99px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Approximately&#x000a0;5% of pineal region masses</p></list-item><list-item><p>10% of intracranial germ cell tumors&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 119.438px; height: 99px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>&#x02191; CSF and plasma&#x000a0;&#x003b2;-hCG&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r13">[13]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>WHO 9100/3 (malignant)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 110px;">
                <td style="width: 27px; height: 110px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">7</td>
                <td style="width: 132px; height: 110px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Embryonal carcinoma</td>
                <td style="width: 125px; height: 110px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Variable</p></list-item><list-item><p>Rare</p></list-item><list-item><p>Mixed germ cell tumors worsen prognosis</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 97.5625px; height: 110px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>&#x0003c;5% of pineal region masses</p></list-item><list-item><p>10% of intracranial germ cell tumors&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r14">[14]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 119.438px; height: 110px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>CSF Markers: &#x02191; AFP; &#x02191;&#x000a0;&#x003b2;-hCG&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r14">[14]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>WHO 9070/3 (malignant)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 113px;">
                <td style="width: 27px; height: 113px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8</td>
                <td style="width: 132px; height: 113px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Yolk sac tumor (endodermal sinus)</td>
                <td style="width: 125px; height: 113px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Rare</p></list-item><list-item><p>May be associated with trisomy 21 (Down syndrome)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r17">[17]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 97.5625px; height: 113px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>One of the rarest intracranial germ cell tumors</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 119.438px; height: 113px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>&#x000a0;CSF: &#x02191;&#x000a0;AFP (nondiagnostic)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r15">[15]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>WHO 9071/3 (malignant)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 73px;">
                <td style="width: 27px; height: 73px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9</td>
                <td style="width: 132px; height: 73px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Immature teratoma</td>
                <td style="width: 125px; height: 73px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Fetal/neonatal</p></list-item><list-item><p>Immature teratomas are more aggressive</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 97.5625px; height: 73px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>26%-50% of fetal brain tumors&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r19">[19]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 119.438px; height: 73px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>&#x000a0;WHO 9080/3 (malignant)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 86px;">
                <td style="width: 27px; height: 86px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10</td>
                <td style="width: 132px; height: 86px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Mature teratoma</td>
                <td style="width: 125px; height: 86px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Fetal/neonatal</p></list-item><list-item><p>Mature teratomas have a favorable prognosis&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r19">[19]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 97.5625px; height: 86px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>26%-50% of fetal brain tumors&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 119.438px; height: 86px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Gross total resection is curative</p></list-item><list-item><p>WHO 9080/0 (benign)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Abbreviations:&#x000a0;CSF,&#x000a0;cerebrospinal fluid;&#x000a0;AFP, alpha-fetoprotein;&#x000a0;hCG,&#x000a0;human chorionic gonadotropin; WHO, World Health Organization; M:F, Male:Female.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27231.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>The pineal gland&#x000a0;develops during the seventh week of gestation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;In humans,&#x000a0;its principal function is the production of melatonin, a hormone that regulates circadian rhythms and sleep-wake cycles. The gland is also involved in regulating puberty and reproductive functions.</p>
        <p>Tumors of the pineal region are diverse in origin due to the proximity to multiple anatomical structures. The pineal gland commonly sequesters embryonic germ cells, which explains why germ cell tumors represent some of the most common pathologies in this location.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r21">[21]</xref></p>
        <p>Germ cell tumors typically occur sporadically.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r22">[22]</xref> However,&#x000a0;they have been associated with certain genetic and syndromic conditions, including&#x000a0;Klinefelter syndrome, Down syndrome, and neurofibromatosis type 1.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r25">[25]</xref></p>
        <p>Pineal region tumors are generally classified into the following groups:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Pineal parenchymal tumors</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Germ cell tumors</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Tumors arising from adjacent cells, such as astrocytes and meningiomas from arachnoid cap cells</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Metastatic tumors</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27231.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>The histopathology of pineal region tumors is highly heterogeneous, reflecting the diverse cellular origins within and around the gland. Tumors may arise from pineal parenchymal cells, germ cells, or adjacent structures, each with distinct morphologic and immunohistochemical profiles. Accurate histopathological classification is essential for guiding prognosis and therapeutic strategies.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Pineocytoma</bold>
</p>
        <p>Pineocytomas are comprised of small cells&#x000a0;resembling&#x000a0;normal pinealocytes, typically arranged in sheets.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;A characteristic histological feature is the presence of pineocytomatous pseudorosettes, which are absent in normal, healthy pineal gland tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;Pineocytomas&#x000a0;lack a well-formed blood-brain barrier, resulting in vivid contrast enhancement on imaging.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;Immunohistochemical staining demonstrates positivity for synaptophysin, neurofilament protein, and neuron-specific enolase. Other neuronal markers, including Tau protein, chromogranin-A, and 5-hydroxytryptamine, may show variable positivity, while neuronal nuclei staining is characteristically negative.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Pineal Parenchymal Tumors with Intermediate Differentiation</bold>
</p>
        <p>These tumors display 2 characteristic microscopic growth patterns&#x02014;lobulated or diffuse.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;The lobulated pattern shows poorly defined lobules separated by large fibrous blood vessels. In contrast, the diffuse pattern&#x000a0;resembles that of oligodendrogliomas or central neurocytomas, and the large pineocytomatous rosettes&#x000a0;typical of pineocytomas are absent. Immunohistochemical staining typically demonstrates positivity for synaptophysin, variable expression of neurofilament protein, and negativity for neuronal nuclei.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Papillary Tumor of the Pineal Region</bold>
</p>
        <p>Papillary tumors of the pineal region&#x000a0;exhibit a variable morphology, ranging from solid to predominantly papillary growth patterns. The histological appearance of these tumors often resembles that of ependymomas and may include ependymal rosettes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;Foci of necrosis are sometimes&#x000a0;observed within the tumor.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;Immunohistochemical staining typically shows positivity for cytokeratins, such as AE1/3, CAM5.2, KL1, and CK18; S100; vimentin; transthyretin; neuron-specific enolase; and microtubule-associated protein 2. Glial fibrillary acidic protein demonstrates variable expression.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Pineoblastoma</bold>
</p>
        <p>Pineoblastomas are composed of&#x000a0;densely packed small, round blue cells with a high nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio. Histologically, Homer Wright and Flexner-Wintersteiner rosettes are occasionally observed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Tumor&#x000a0;necrosis&#x000a0;is common, and the mitotic rate is typically high.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Immunohistochemically, pineoblastomas demonstrate positivity for&#x000a0;synaptophysin and SMARCB1, whereas other neuronal markers, including neuron-specific enolase, neurofilament protein, and chromogranin-A, show variable expression.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Germinoma</bold>
</p>
        <p>Germinomas originate from primordial germ cells and&#x000a0;share morphological features with their gonadal counterparts. The tumor cells&#x000a0;exhibit&#x000a0;large nuclei with prominent nucleoli, and dense lymphocytic infiltration is frequently present. Immunohistochemically, the cells are positive for placental alkaline phosphatase.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;CSF analysis may reveal elevated&#x000a0;placental alkaline phosphatase and &#x003b2;-hCG.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r10">[10]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27231.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>The clinical presentation of pineal region tumors is mainly related to obstructive hydrocephalus and compression of the tectum or dorsal midbrain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;Consequently, regardless of the pathology, the physical examination findings&#x000a0;are similar&#x000a0;across the different tumor types.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hydrocephalus:&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <list list-type="bullet">
              <list-item>
                <p>History: Headache, nausea, vomiting, failure to thrive, macrocephaly, blurry vision, lethargy, drowsiness, or coma.</p>
              </list-item>
            </list>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Examination:</p></list-item><list-item><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Cushing triad: Hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respirations&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Bilateral optic nerve swelling or papilledema</p></list-item><list-item><p>Bilateral sixth nerve palsy</p></list-item></list></list-item></list>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Compression of the tectum or dorsal midbrain (Parinaud syndrome):</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <list list-type="bullet">
              <list-item>
                <p>History: Difficulty walking up stairs and diplopia</p>
              </list-item>
            </list>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Examination:</p></list-item><list-item><list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Paralysis of upward gaze&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;conjugate downward gaze due to compression of the vertical gaze center at the rostral interstitial nucleus of the medial longitudinal fasciculus</p></list-item><list-item><p>Light-near dissociation from compression of&#x000a0;the pretectal nucleus in the superior colliculus</p></list-item><list-item><p>Convergence-retraction nystagmus and eyelid retraction (Collier sign) caused by damage to the supranuclear fibers of the third nerve at the posterior midbrain</p></list-item></list></list-item></list>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27231.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>The evaluation of pineal region tumors requires a multimodal approach that integrates clinical, radiologic, and laboratory findings. Neuroimaging, particularly magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) with contrast, is central to identifying the tumor and its relationship to adjacent structures. In contrast, CSF fluid analysis and serum tumor markers help differentiate germ cell tumors from other pineal lesions. Hisopathological confirmation remains essential for definitive diagnosis and treatment planning.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Laboratory Evaluation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Serum tumor markers are obtained when specific tumors are suspected, particularly those of the germ cell type. The most commonly assessed markers include AFP, &#x003b2;-hCG, and placental alkaline phosphatase.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;These markers provide important diagnostic and prognostic information and may help distinguish between germinomatous and nongerminatous tumors.</p>
        <p>CSF&#x000a0;analysis can be performed&#x000a0;via lumbar puncture, provided there is no obstructive hydrocephalus, or during endoscopic procedures. CSF evaluation helps diagnose many tumors of the pineal region and can provide complementary information to serum markers and imaging findings (see <bold>Image.&#x000a0;</bold>Tumor Markers).</p>
        <p>
<bold>Imaging Studies</bold>
</p>
        <p>Brain MRI&#x000a0;with and without gadolinium enhancement is the gold standard for evaluating pineal region tumors.&#x000a0;When&#x000a0;MRI is not available, alternative imaging modalities such as head computed tomographic (CT) scan, angiography, and, in infants, cranial ultrasound can play a role in the assessment of&#x000a0;pineal region mass lesions.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Normal neuroimaging findings of the pineal gland:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>CT scan:&#x000a0;Calcification of the pineal gland is a common finding in adults but is typically absent in children&#x000a0;younger than 5. The prevalence of this calcification peaks around the age of 30.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r31">[31]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>MRI:&#x000a0;On MRI, the pineal gland demonstrates signal intensity similar to that of gray matter and appears as a small nodule of tissue located in&#x000a0;the posterior aspect of the third ventricle.&#x000a0;Because the gland lacks&#x000a0;a&#x000a0;blood-brain barrier, it shows avid enhancement with gadolinium. Calcifications are also frequently visualized on MRI.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r20">[20]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Brain MRI shows homogeneous enhancement in pineal region tumors, except in cases with heterogeneous cellularity. Dense tumors, such as pineoblastomas and germinomas, often exhibit restricted diffusion on diffusion-weighted imaging.&#x000a0;In contrast, pineal cysts typically lack enhancement and&#x000a0;are characterized by a thin wall. Therefore, the presence of a thick lesion wall&#x000a0;excludes the diagnosis of a cyst.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Calcifications are&#x000a0;more readily&#x000a0;identified on head CT and can sometimes assist in establishing an accurate diagnosis. Pineal parenchymal tumors such as pineocytoma&#x000a0;and pineoblastomas typically demonstrate peripheral calcifications, whereas germ cell tumors tend to encompass the calcified pineal tissue.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Many tumors of the pineal region may spread locally or seed distantly.&#x000a0;Therefore, a complete spinal MRI&#x000a0;is often recommended as a part of the initial evaluation. Evidence of local invasion&#x000a0;or remote&#x000a0;dissemination carries&#x000a0;important&#x000a0;prognostic implications and&#x000a0;can significantly&#x000a0;alter&#x000a0;both initial management and&#x000a0;the selection of adjuvant&#x000a0;therapy.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Prognosis Indicators</bold>
</p>
        <p>Findings&#x000a0;associated with a&#x000a0;worse prognosis include local invasion, CSF seeding, and extensive peritumoral edema. These features can significantly impact survival outcomes and influence the treatment approach.</p>
        <p>Local invasion is most often observed in pineoblastomas and many germ cell tumors. The presence of local invasion reflects aggressive biological behavior and can complicate surgical resection.</p>
        <p>CSF seeding is another indicator of poor prognosis. Pineoblastomas most frequently demonstrate distal dissemination, whereas germinomas are more likely to exhibit proximal seeding.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27231.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Following established treatment algorithms is essential for the evaluation of pineal region tumors. These protocols ensure both accurate diagnosis and appropriate management.&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>If hydrocephalus is absent:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Obtain tumor markers&#x000a0;in serum and CSF
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>If positive, treatment should&#x000a0;proceed based on the most likely pathology&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>If negative, tissue diagnosis is required
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Biopsy (stereotactic, endoscopic, open)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Resection (guided by&#x000a0;radiographic or preliminary pathology findings)</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>The confirmed pathology determines final management</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>If hydrocephalus is present:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>CSF&#x000a0;diversion should be performed, with endoscopic third ventriculostomy (ETV) being the preferred approach.&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Always&#x000a0;attempt an endoscopic biopsy when performing an ETV</p></list-item><list-item><p>Final management is based on pathology</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Endoscopic Third Ventriculostomy</bold>
</p>
        <p>ETV is performed by&#x000a0;fenestrating the&#x000a0;floor of the third ventricle, thereby&#x000a0;creating a passage between the third ventricle and the prepontine cistern. In most cases, a right frontal burr hole is made anterior to Kocher's point, and the neuroendoscope is advanced through the brain parenchyma&#x000a0;into the ipsilateral lateral ventricle. From there, the floor of the third ventricle near the tuber cinereum is fenestrated and expanded to permit adequate CSF flow.</p>
        <p>If abnormal tissue is visualized, a biopsy is obtained from the posterior aspect of the third ventricle. Should ETV fail, definitive treatment involves placement of a ventricular shunt.</p>
        <p>The ETV success score is calculated using 3 variables&#x02014;age, cause of hydrocephalus, and prior ventricular shunt placement. Each variable is assigned a success percentage, which is added to yield a total score ranging from 0% to 90% (see <bold>Table. </bold>Endoscopic&#x000a0;Third&#x000a0;Ventriculostomy&#x000a0;Success Score).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r32">[32]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-27231.table1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 2. Endoscopic&#x000a0;Third&#x000a0;Ventriculostomy&#x000a0;Success Score</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 600px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 46px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>#</bold>
</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 399px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Variable</bold>
</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 273.292px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Success Score (%)</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 46px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 399px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Age &#x0003c;1 month</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 273.292px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 46px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 399px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Age 1-6 months</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 273.292px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 46px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 399px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Age 6 months&#x000a0;to 1 year</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 273.292px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">30</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 46px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 399px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Age 1-10 years</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 273.292px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">40</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 46px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 399px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Age &#x0003e;10 years</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 273.292px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">50</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 46px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 399px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cause: Postinfectious</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 273.292px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 46px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">7</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 399px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cause: Myelomeningocele, intraventricular hemorrhage, nontectal tumor</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 273.292px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 46px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 399px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cause: Tectal tumor, aqueductal stenosis, other</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 273.292px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">30</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 46px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 399px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Previous shunt: Yes</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 273.292px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 46px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 399px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Previous shunt: No</td>
