              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<bold>Treatment</bold>
</p>
        <p>The underlying pathology guides definitive treatment of pineal region tumors. Surgical resection is the mainstay for all pineal parenchymal tumors, mature teratomas, and for residual disease following chemotherapy and radiotherapy, in what is termed second-look surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r33">[33]</xref></p>
        <p>Radiotherapy is primarily used in the treatment of germinomas but is generally reserved for patients older than 3. In younger patients, alternative approaches are preferred to minimize long-term neurocognitive effects.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Chemotherapy combined with radiotherapy represents the standard for most other pineal region tumors. This approach is indicated for all nongerminomatous germ cell tumors and for germinomatous germ cell tumors in children younger than 3. Adjuvant chemotherapy with radiotherapy is also recommended for all tumors, except pineocytomas and mature teratomas, when complete resection has been achieved.</p>
        <p>The surgical approach to the pineal region is&#x000a0;determined based&#x000a0;on the tumor's location and the surgeon's preference. The paramedian infratentorial supracerebellar approach is considered the most flexible option for resection of the most pineal region tumors, except for large lesions that extend laterally and inferiorly.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r34">[34]</xref>&#x000a0;This technique involves a left-sided suboccipital craniotomy, which protects&#x000a0;the torcula and the typically more dominant right-sided veins and dural sinuses, including the transverse sinus.</p>
        <p>The midline infratentorial supracerebellar approach has traditionally been used to expose pineal region tumors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r35">[35]</xref>&#x000a0;This technique&#x000a0;is performed via a bilateral midline suboccipital craniotomy but is limited by restricted lateral and inferior visualization due to the tentorial angle and the obstructive apex of the culmen. In addition, nearly all midline bridging vermian veins must be sacrificed.</p>
        <p>The occipital transtentorial approach is beneficial for large pineal region tumors that extend laterally and inferiorly.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r37">[37]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r38">[38]</xref>&#x000a0;This technique is performed through a unilateral occipital craniotomy. Limitations include&#x000a0;challenges with anatomic orientation, the need to divide the tentorium, and the&#x000a0;risk of homonymous hemianopia&#x000a0;resulting from occipital lobe retraction.</p>
        <p>Screening of the entire neuroaxis is essential in all patients with pineal region tumors. Prognosis and treatment vary dramatically depending on&#x000a0;whether CSF dissemination is present.&#x000a0;Distant CSF seeding, except germinoma, and drop metastases&#x000a0;are associated with poor outcomes. Comprehensive screening requires MRI of the brain, as well as the cervical, thoracic, and lumbar spine, with and without contrast. The role of prophylactic spinal radiotherapy&#x000a0;remains controversial.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27231.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis of pineal region tumors is broad, reflecting the variety of neoplastic and nonneoplastic lesions that may arise in this location. Careful consideration of clinical presentation, imaging characteristics, and histopathology is essential to distinguish these entities and guide appropriate management.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Cystic nonneoplastic lesions:</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <list list-type="bullet">
              <list-item>
                <p>Pineal cysts</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Cavum veli interpositum</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Arachnoid cyst</p>
              </list-item>
            </list>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Pineal parenchymal tumors:</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <list list-type="bullet">
              <list-item>
                <p>Pineocytoma</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Pineal parenchymal tumor with intermediate differentiation</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Pineoblastoma</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Papillary tumor of the pineal region</p>
              </list-item>
            </list>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Germ cell tumors:</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <list list-type="bullet">
              <list-item>
                <p>Pineal germinoma</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Embryonal carcinoma</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Choriocarcinoma</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Teratoma (mature/immature)</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Yolk sac tumor</p>
              </list-item>
            </list>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Tumors also encountered in the pineal region:</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <list list-type="bullet">
              <list-item>
                <p>Astrocytoma</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Meningioma</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Cerebral metastases</p>
              </list-item>
            </list>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Pineal gland metastases</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Vascular lesions:</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <list list-type="bullet">
              <list-item>
                <p>Vein of Galen aneurysm/malformation</p>
              </list-item>
              <list-item>
                <p>Internal cerebral vein thrombosis</p>
              </list-item>
            </list>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27231.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis of pineal region tumors varies widely (see <bold>Table.&#x000a0;</bold>Prognosis Summary of Pineal Region Tumors)&#x000a0;and is closely linked to tumor histology, extent of resection, and presence of CSF dissemination. Advances in neuroimaging, microsurgical techniques, and multimodal therapy have improved survival outcomes, although specific aggressive subtypes continue to carry a poor prognosis.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-27231.table2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 4.&#x000a0;Prognosis Summary of Pineal Region Tumors</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 23px;">
                <td style="width: 39px; height: 23px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>#</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 159.51px; height: 23px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Tumor Type</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 313.49px; height: 23px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Prognosis/Survival</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 312px; height: 23px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Prognostic Factors</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 54px;">
                <td style="width: 39px; height: 54px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td>
                <td style="width: 159.51px; height: 54px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pineocytoma</td>
                <td style="width: 313.49px; height: 54px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>5-year survival: 86%-91%&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r4">[4]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Excellent if completely resected</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 312px; height: 54px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Complete surgical resection</p></list-item><list-item><p>Rare local recurrence or CSF spread&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r7">[7]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 55px;">
                <td style="width: 39px; height: 55px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2</td>
                <td style="width: 159.51px; height: 55px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Pineal parenchymal tumor with intermediate differentiation</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 313.49px; height: 55px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Median overall survival: 165 months</p></list-item><list-item><p>Progression-free survival: 93 months&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r4">[4]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 312px; height: 55px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>More localized disease than pineoblastoma</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 50px;">
                <td style="width: 39px; height: 50px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3</td>
                <td style="width: 159.51px; height: 50px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pineoblastoma</td>
                <td style="width: 313.49px; height: 50px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Median overall survival: 4-8 years&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r4">[4]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>5-year overall survival: 10%-81%</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 312px; height: 50px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Worse with CSF spread, young age, or incomplete resection</p></list-item><list-item><p>Improved with chemotherapy&#x000a0;&#x000b1; stem cells&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r9">[9]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 97px;">
                <td style="width: 39px; height: 97px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4</td>
                <td style="width: 159.51px; height: 97px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Papillary tumor of the pineal region</td>
                <td style="width: 313.49px; height: 97px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>5-year overall survival: 73%</p></list-item><list-item><p>5-year progression-free survival: 27%&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r4">[4]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 312px; height: 97px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Better with gross total resection and younger age</p></list-item><list-item><p>Local recurrence common</p></list-item><list-item><p>CSF spread in 7%</p></list-item><list-item><p>Radiotherapy and chemotherapy had no significant impact on survival&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r4">[4]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48.6667px;">
                <td style="width: 39px; height: 48.6667px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5</td>
                <td style="width: 159.51px; height: 48.6667px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Germinoma</td>
                <td style="width: 313.49px; height: 48.6667px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>&#x0003e;90% long-term survival with craniospinal irradiation&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r39">[39]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 312px; height: 48.6667px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Highly radiosensitive</p></list-item><list-item><p>Leptomeningeal/intracentricular spread in 13%&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="width: 39px; height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6</td>
                <td style="width: 159.51px; height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Mature teratoma</td>
                <td style="width: 313.49px; height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Curable with complete excision&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r4">[4]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 312px; height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Excellent if gross total resection is achieved&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 56px;">
                <td style="width: 39px; height: 56px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">7</td>
                <td style="width: 159.51px; height: 56px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Immature teratoma</td>
                <td style="width: 313.49px; height: 56px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Intermediate behavior</p></list-item><list-item><p>Prognosis depends on size and location</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 312px; height: 56px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Dependent on tumor size and location&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r4">[4]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 59px;">
                <td style="width: 39px; height: 59px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8</td>
                <td style="width: 159.51px; height: 59px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Embryonal carcinoma, choriocarcinoma, and yolk sac tumor</td>
                <td style="width: 313.49px; height: 59px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Survival 60%-70% with chemotherapy + radiation therapy</p></list-item></list>
</td>
                <td style="width: 312px; height: 59px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Aggressive&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27231.r4">[4]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Recurrence and CSF dissemination are common</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>&#x000a0;Abbreviation: CSF, cerebrospinal fluid.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27231.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications of pineal region&#x000a0;tumors arise from both the tumor itself and its treatment, including neurological deficits, endocrine dysfunction, and treatment-related morbidity, all of which can significantly affect long-term outcomes and quality of life. Complications include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Tumor-related complications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Hydrocephalus</p></list-item><list-item><p>Parinaud syndrome</p></list-item><list-item><p>Raised intracranial pressure</p></list-item><list-item><p>Neurological deficits</p></list-item><list-item><p>CSF dissemination</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Treatment-related complications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Surgical complications
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Hemorrhage</p></list-item><list-item><p>Venous infarction</p></list-item><list-item><p>Visual deficits</p></list-item><list-item><p>Infection</p></list-item><list-item><p>Cerebellar ataxia</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Radiotherapy effects
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Cognitive decline</p></list-item><list-item><p>Endocrine dysfunction (hypothalamic-pituitary axis damage)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Secondary malignancies</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Chemotherapy toxicities
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Myelosuppresion</p></list-item><list-item><p>Nephrotoxicity</p></list-item><list-item><p>Ototoxicity</p></list-item><list-item><p>Infertility</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Shunt/ETV complications&#x000a0;
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Shunt dependency</p></list-item><list-item><p>Infection</p></list-item><list-item><p>Obstruction</p></list-item><list-item><p>Failure of CSF diversion</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27231.s13" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>The following specialty consultations&#x000a0;are recommended for the comprehensive diagnosis and management of pineal gland tumors:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Neurosurgeon</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Neurologist</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pediatrician</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Endocrinologist</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Opthalmology</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hematology oncologist</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Radiation oncologist</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27231.s14" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Pineal region tumors may recur even after long disease-free intervals, underscoring the need for prolonged follow-up. Patients with cerebral CSF shunts require lifelong monitoring to detect shunt-related complications. As prognosis and treatment are closely tied to tumor histology, patients and families must receive counseling about the specific tumor type and the potential need for adjuvant therapy.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27231.s15" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Key facts to keep in mind about gland tumors include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Geminoma is the most common pineal tumor; it is highly radiosensitive and has an excellent prognosis.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pineoblastoma is an aggressive tumor that occurs in children and is linked to bilateral retinoblastoma (trilateral retinoblastoma).</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Classic presentation includes hydrocephalus and Parinaud syndrome (upward gaze palsy, light-near dissociation).</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>AFP and&#x000a0;&#x003b2;-hCG are key markers (elevated in nongerminomatous germ cell tumors;&#x000a0;&#x003b2;-hCG sometimes in germinomas).</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>MRI is the gold standard.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Calcification pattern on MRI
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Peripheral=parenchymal tumor</p></list-item><list-item><p>Engulfed=germ cell tumor</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27231.s16" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Optimal management of pineal region tumors requires an interprofessional approach that emphasizes not only clinical expertise but also effective communication, shared responsibility, and ethical collaboration. Clinicians and advanced practitioners provide diagnostic accuracy, surgical and medical management, and evidence-based decision-making. Nurses play a critical role in perioperative and postoperative care, patient education, and monitoring for complications, ensuring safety and continuity of care. Pharmacists contribute through precise chemotherapy and supportive medication management, reducing risks of adverse effects and improving treatment adherence.</p>
        <p>Neuroradiologists and cytologists support the team by providing diagnostic clarity through advanced imaging interpretation and tumor marker identification, which are essential for tailoring treatment strategies. Equally important is the commitment to interprofessional communication, which includes regular multidisciplinary meetings, transparent documentation, and shared decision-making with patients and families, fostering trust and aligning care goals. Ethical responsibilities include respecting patient autonomy, addressing long-term quality of life, and ensuring equitable access to care. By integrating these strategies, the healthcare team enhances patient-centered outcomes, minimizes complications, improves safety, and strengthens overall team performance in the complex management of pineal region tumors.</p>
      </sec>
